1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,860
It's two fifty-eight on a Friday.

2
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:03,360
Maths.

3
00:00:03,560 --> 00:00:05,800
The clock on the wall is barely moving.

4
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:07,860
You stare at it and think, just be

5
00:00:07,860 --> 00:00:08,620
over already.

6
00:00:09,480 --> 00:00:09,860
You blink.

7
00:00:10,140 --> 00:00:12,440
And it's three fifteen.

8
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:13,360
The classroom is empty.

9
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:14,420
Your bag is packed.

10
00:00:14,780 --> 00:00:16,680
The bell rang, and you weren't there for

11
00:00:16,680 --> 00:00:16,880
it.

12
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:18,200
Your friend is in the doorway.

13
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:19,980
Why are you just sitting there?

14
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:22,240
You've been staring at the wall for twenty

15
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:22,540
minutes!

16
00:00:23,260 --> 00:00:24,020
You weren't staring.

17
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:25,320
You were skipping.

18
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:27,440
Over the next few days you work it

19
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:27,600
out.

20
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:29,720
You can jump forward.

21
00:00:29,720 --> 00:00:32,840
A minute, an hour, a day.

22
00:00:32,840 --> 00:00:35,720
But never, ever back.

23
00:00:35,720 --> 00:00:38,100
So what if you could only travel forward

24
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:38,720
in time?

25
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:40,020
Here's the twist.

26
00:00:40,020 --> 00:00:41,560
You already do.

27
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:44,700
At first, it's the best power in the

28
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:45,140
world.

29
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:45,920
The dentist?

30
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:46,560
Skip.

31
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:47,720
The queue for the bus?

32
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:48,280
Skip.

33
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:49,600
Waiting for the pizza?

34
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:51,800
Skip, and it's hot on the table.

35
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:54,720
Then your mum asks why your homework is

36
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:55,160
never done.

37
00:00:56,120 --> 00:00:56,760
And that's the catch.

38
00:00:57,360 --> 00:00:59,300
When you skip the boring part, you also

39
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:01,400
skip the time you needed to do it.

40
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:03,100
Your friend tries to understand.

41
00:01:03,860 --> 00:01:04,340
Where do you go?

42
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:05,460
Nowhere.

43
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:06,500
I just get there later.

44
00:01:07,040 --> 00:01:09,260
And that's exactly how real time travel works.

45
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:12,980
A hundred years ago, Einstein worked out something

46
00:01:12,980 --> 00:01:13,740
wild.

47
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:16,660
The faster you move, the slower your clock

48
00:01:16,660 --> 00:01:18,740
ticks, compared with someone standing still.

49
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:20,520
It sounds like science fiction.

50
00:01:21,060 --> 00:01:24,320
So in 1971, two scientists put four atomic

51
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:26,700
clocks on passenger planes and flew them around

52
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:27,080
the world.

53
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:30,000
When they landed, the clocks no longer agreed

54
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:31,200
with the ones that stayed behind.

55
00:01:31,740 --> 00:01:33,320
By billionths of a second.

56
00:01:33,740 --> 00:01:35,220
Exactly as Einstein predicted.

57
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:37,620
Your phone uses this every day.

58
00:01:37,960 --> 00:01:40,000
The clocks on GPS satellites drift by about

59
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:41,980
thirty-eight millionths of a second a day.

60
00:01:42,520 --> 00:01:43,300
Doesn't sound like much.

61
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:45,520
But if engineers didn't correct for it, your

62
00:01:45,520 --> 00:01:47,500
map would be off by about ten kilometres

63
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:48,120
by tomorrow.

64
00:01:49,180 --> 00:01:51,660
Some people have actually travelled into the future.

65
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:54,880
Cosmonaut Sergei Krikalev spent eight hundred and three

66
00:01:54,880 --> 00:01:55,880
days in space.

67
00:01:56,240 --> 00:01:58,160
Moving that fast for that long, he landed

68
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:00,220
about two hundredths of a second in the

69
00:02:00,220 --> 00:02:00,500
future.

70
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:02,540
And on one trip, he went up in

71
00:02:02,540 --> 00:02:04,700
May 1991 for about five months.

72
00:02:05,140 --> 00:02:06,360
He stayed for ten.

73
00:02:06,940 --> 00:02:08,460
While he was up there, the country that

74
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:11,500
launched him, the Soviet Union, stopped existing.

75
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:13,700
He went up from one country, and came

76
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:15,000
down to a different one.

77
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:18,100
Two hundredths of a second isn't going to

78
00:02:18,100 --> 00:02:18,880
get you out of maths.

79
00:02:19,480 --> 00:02:21,440
The fastest any human has ever travelled is

80
00:02:21,440 --> 00:02:23,140
Apollo 10, coming back from the Moon.

81
00:02:23,540 --> 00:02:25,780
Almost forty thousand kilometres an hour.

82
00:02:26,420 --> 00:02:29,480
That's less than one two-hundredth of one percent

83
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:30,640
of the speed of light.

84
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:32,780
To really skip ahead, you need to get

85
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:33,800
close to light speed.

86
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:36,120
At ninety-nine percent of it, every year on

87
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:38,040
your ship is about seven years back on

88
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:38,220
Earth.

89
00:02:38,760 --> 00:02:40,540
So you leave at thirteen.

90
00:02:40,540 --> 00:02:41,720
You come back a year older.

91
00:02:41,720 --> 00:02:44,460
Your friend is twenty.

92
00:02:44,460 --> 00:02:45,460
Here's the problem.

93
00:02:45,880 --> 00:02:47,900
Getting just your body to that speed takes

94
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:50,660
about thirty-eight billion billion joules of energy.

95
00:02:51,160 --> 00:02:53,720
That's around six percent of all the energy

96
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:55,580
humanity uses in a whole year.

97
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:56,700
Just for you.

98
00:02:57,040 --> 00:02:57,640
Not the ship.

99
00:02:58,440 --> 00:03:00,180
Even pushing at a steady one g, it

100
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:01,860
takes about two and a half years just

101
00:03:01,860 --> 00:03:02,560
to get up to speed.

102
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:04,540
And space isn't empty.

103
00:03:04,980 --> 00:03:06,680
There are one or two hydrogen atoms in

104
00:03:06,680 --> 00:03:08,520
every sugar-cube-sized bit of it.

105
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:10,980
Near light speed, those atoms hit you like

106
00:03:10,980 --> 00:03:12,220
a beam of radiation.

107
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:15,160
One physicist calculated it could be deadly in

108
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:15,840
about a second.

109
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:17,140
Honestly?

110
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:19,260
Nobody has solved that yet.

111
00:03:20,520 --> 00:03:22,100
There's a second way to skip ahead.

112
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:23,040
Gravity.

113
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:25,500
The closer you are to something heavy, the

114
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:27,040
slower your time runs.

115
00:03:27,620 --> 00:03:30,060
In 2010, scientists in the United States raised

116
00:03:30,060 --> 00:03:33,420
an atomic clock just thirty-three centimetres.

117
00:03:33,420 --> 00:03:34,720
And it ticked faster.

118
00:03:35,220 --> 00:03:37,440
That means your head is ageing faster than

119
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:37,980
your feet.

120
00:03:37,980 --> 00:03:41,340
By about ninety billionths of a second over

121
00:03:41,340 --> 00:03:42,060
a whole lifetime.

122
00:03:42,540 --> 00:03:42,860
Don't worry.

123
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:43,940
You won't notice.

124
00:03:45,100 --> 00:03:46,360
But you get greedy.

125
00:03:46,940 --> 00:03:47,860
Exam week is coming.

126
00:03:48,260 --> 00:03:50,180
So you close your eyes, and you push.

127
00:03:50,180 --> 00:03:51,300
Harder than ever.

128
00:03:51,860 --> 00:03:53,480
When you open them, the lake is the

129
00:03:53,480 --> 00:03:53,700
same.

130
00:03:54,260 --> 00:03:55,700
The pier is new.

131
00:03:56,340 --> 00:03:57,720
Your phone has no signal.

132
00:03:58,060 --> 00:03:58,960
It won't even turn on.

133
00:03:59,660 --> 00:04:01,540
Someone is standing at the end of the

134
00:04:01,540 --> 00:04:01,780
pier.

135
00:04:02,700 --> 00:04:02,960
Taller.

136
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:03,680
A beard.

137
00:04:04,180 --> 00:04:04,680
A blue cap.

138
00:04:05,080 --> 00:04:07,160
He stares at you like he's seeing a

139
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:07,460
ghost.

140
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:09,520
You've been gone ten years.

141
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:11,120
Ten years of birthdays.

142
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:13,160
Ten years of everybody looking for you.

143
00:04:13,420 --> 00:04:15,400
And there's no button that takes you back.

144
00:04:16,720 --> 00:04:18,000
So is backwards possible?

145
00:04:18,580 --> 00:04:21,080
Einstein's equations allow some strange loops in time,

146
00:04:21,080 --> 00:04:21,720
on paper.

147
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:23,760
But nobody knows how to build one.

148
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:26,700
Stephen Hawking even suggested that nature itself might

149
00:04:26,700 --> 00:04:27,420
forbid it.

150
00:04:27,840 --> 00:04:29,400
It's an idea, not a proof.

151
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:32,280
Right now, forward is the only direction we

152
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:32,760
know works.

153
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:35,520
Your friend sits down next to you.

154
00:04:35,520 --> 00:04:36,740
So, he says.

155
00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:38,240
Was it worth skipping maths?

156
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:40,180
So what would you skip, if you could

157
00:04:40,180 --> 00:04:40,900
only go forward?

158
00:04:41,620 --> 00:04:42,340
Tell us in the comments.

159
00:04:42,340 --> 00:04:45,040
And subscribe, because next time, you find out

160
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:47,000
what happens if you could stop time completely.
